1
00:00:03,544 --> 00:00:11,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you say that the Sotāpanna is perfectly self-transparent?

2
00:00:16,354 --> 00:00:18,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that way is the Sotāpanna.

3
00:00:21,137 --> 00:00:24,060
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view is basically self-transparency?

4
00:00:26,481 --> 00:00:27,943
[SPEAKER_00]: To the sufficient extent.

5
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:32,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically...

6
00:00:32,551 --> 00:00:34,572
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the culmination of... Yeah, yeah.

7
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:42,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the art of complete culmination is arahantship, when there's absolutely no contradiction left within you.

8
00:00:43,197 --> 00:00:50,821
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what Buddha meant by the little dust in their eyes, more dust in their eyes.

9
00:00:50,842 --> 00:00:55,304
[SPEAKER_01]: What is basically transparent means you see through clearly.

10
00:00:56,825 --> 00:00:57,966
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing obscuring.

11
00:01:00,678 --> 00:01:05,002
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not as transparent, you have some dust in your eyes, so you cannot quite see clearly.

12
00:01:09,465 --> 00:01:11,667
[SPEAKER_00]: There's someone with a little bit of dust in their eyes.

13
00:01:11,687 --> 00:01:13,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Means they could still hear him.

14
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:13,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

15
00:01:13,969 --> 00:01:15,971
[SPEAKER_00]: They could still recognize what he was talking about.

16
00:01:16,071 --> 00:01:19,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the ones with a little dust in their eyes, yeah, they would need just

17
00:01:20,672 --> 00:01:35,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Fewer prompts towards that... oh, developing that transparency, that recognition of the certain universal principles, understanding those, understanding the nature of things, to the fundamental, to their own existence, and that's it.

18
00:01:36,721 --> 00:01:38,122
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing else that's being obscured.

19
00:01:38,923 --> 00:01:44,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have those with more dust in their eyes, they need to invest more effort into developing their transparency.

20
00:01:49,188 --> 00:01:52,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, the question is what transparency is?

21
00:01:52,791 --> 00:01:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

22
00:01:53,832 --> 00:01:56,715
[SPEAKER_00]: How does somebody... What is self-transparency?

23
00:01:56,775 --> 00:02:14,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And, see, there is... developing self-transparency is a thread of practice, of effort, that is in common to somebody who is patujana and somebody who is even ādi-asāvaka.

24
00:02:16,809 --> 00:02:25,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if Puthujjana starts to develop self-transparency, he's heading towards the right view, even if he doesn't necessarily know it.

25
00:02:25,334 --> 00:02:30,757
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of his transparency is sort of... that's what's in common?

26
00:02:30,857 --> 00:02:33,519
[SPEAKER_01]: At least the idea of the direction that that's worth developing.

27
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:43,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why some people in the suttas, when they met the Buddha, literally just heard one sentence from him and became fully enlightened, because they've actually

28
00:02:44,870 --> 00:02:55,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like... that's the kind of... that's the basis of the Buddha's teaching, the practice.

29
00:02:56,059 --> 00:03:07,509
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to... if you're interested in practicing according to what the Buddha was talking about, then it's all within this idea of becoming transparent.

30
00:03:07,749 --> 00:03:07,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

31
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:10,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding your motives.

32
00:03:10,972 --> 00:03:11,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

33
00:03:13,373 --> 00:03:14,916
[SPEAKER_01]: I was going to ask you that now.

34
00:03:15,057 --> 00:03:15,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

35
00:03:15,277 --> 00:03:15,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what...

36
00:03:17,548 --> 00:03:20,669
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the definition of transparent and non-transparent?

37
00:03:20,709 --> 00:03:21,430
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the difference?

38
00:03:21,470 --> 00:03:27,992
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be a different characteristic of it, that you could differentiate these two things?

39
00:03:28,132 --> 00:03:31,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody who's transparent has... is clear about their motives.

40
00:03:32,514 --> 00:03:32,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

41
00:03:33,154 --> 00:03:35,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, transparent in regard to what?

42
00:03:35,475 --> 00:03:37,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Transparent in regard to motivation.

43
00:03:37,736 --> 00:03:37,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

44
00:03:37,956 --> 00:03:38,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Motivation for what?

45
00:03:39,456 --> 00:03:43,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, motivation for bodily action, for verbal action, for mental action.

46
00:03:43,338 --> 00:03:43,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

47
00:03:44,863 --> 00:03:50,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the entire teaching is bound and contained within that nature of action and freedom from it.

48
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:51,930
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I doing?

49
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:52,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like...

50
00:03:53,111 --> 00:03:57,316
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not transparent in regard to such and such thing that I don't understand.

51
00:03:57,636 --> 00:03:59,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the transparency we talk about.

52
00:03:59,238 --> 00:03:59,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

53
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:05,984
[SPEAKER_01]: It's transparency in regard to your own present, here and now, pretty much, whatever you're doing, thinking, saying.

54
00:04:06,044 --> 00:04:06,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

55
00:04:06,545 --> 00:04:08,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Transparency in regard to motivation.

56
00:04:08,427 --> 00:04:08,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

57
00:04:10,192 --> 00:04:13,815
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the most encompassing general way you could describe it.

58
00:04:14,296 --> 00:04:19,981
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of like... could you say it's like... yes, it's not being clear about everything, but just being... Far from it.

59
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:20,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

60
00:04:20,381 --> 00:04:20,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Far from it.

61
00:04:20,981 --> 00:04:24,124
[SPEAKER_00]: But just being... because there are things that are not clear, that's their nature.

62
00:04:24,144 --> 00:04:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

63
00:04:25,045 --> 00:04:28,148
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's being clear about the direction you're heading in.

64
00:04:28,839 --> 00:04:29,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

65
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:30,500
[SPEAKER_01]: It's being cleared.

66
00:04:31,081 --> 00:04:35,325
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about being clear about actions you're intending.

67
00:04:35,505 --> 00:04:35,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

68
00:04:35,725 --> 00:04:37,727
[SPEAKER_01]: You're about to intend actions you intended.

69
00:04:37,867 --> 00:04:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's just the kind of vague direction of those actions.

70
00:04:42,511 --> 00:04:43,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in maths, I say vague.

71
00:04:44,112 --> 00:04:54,221
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be... you might not be able to... Like I say, not everything is clear, but you just know that this direction is...

72
00:04:55,488 --> 00:04:56,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the content.

73
00:04:56,689 --> 00:05:00,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, like, shall I do this or shall I do that?

74
00:05:00,292 --> 00:05:01,793
[SPEAKER_01]: I have an option A, an option B.

75
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:07,078
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can investigate the option A, see the drawbacks of option B. I can look at the drawback.

76
00:05:07,098 --> 00:05:10,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is kind of secondary to what I'm talking about.

77
00:05:11,341 --> 00:05:17,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see why are you considering doing either option A or option B, regardless of the results of option A and option B.

78
00:05:17,967 --> 00:05:20,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you cannot see any drawback in an option A,

79
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:25,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still recognize that it's purely rooted in your desire for pleasure.

80
00:05:27,052 --> 00:05:27,432
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

81
00:05:28,292 --> 00:05:28,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't do it.

82
00:05:29,012 --> 00:05:31,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's more important.

83
00:05:31,193 --> 00:05:32,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Where the action is rooted.

84
00:05:33,153 --> 00:05:41,916
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you reflect and contemplate that, the more transparent, in regard to your own motivation, that it's there, while you want to commit the action, you will become.

85
00:05:44,837 --> 00:05:50,039
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it will take you less and less effort to see where the action is rooted.

86
00:05:51,023 --> 00:05:57,605
[SPEAKER_01]: In greed, aversion, delusion, or non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, the more you invest into seeing it.

87
00:06:00,066 --> 00:06:20,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, for a layperson, for example, who is not in the environment of the sensory strain of a monk or somebody who has kind of renounced the world, but who wants to practice the dhamma, so, like, the absolute beginner, so to speak, even that, it's the same principle.

88
00:06:23,804 --> 00:06:34,970
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be clear for the reasons, clear about the reasons that are making you act in that direction or in this direction, choosing this, choosing that.

89
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:42,333
[SPEAKER_01]: And that applies to any form of action, not just immediate one I'm about to do now, but even my future plans.

90
00:06:43,214 --> 00:06:44,494
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, that's another thing.

91
00:06:44,714 --> 00:06:48,696
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a layperson, I want to practice dhamma.

92
00:06:49,477 --> 00:06:49,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

93
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:51,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I want to practice dhamma?

94
00:06:53,031 --> 00:06:55,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I want to be free from suffering.

95
00:06:55,673 --> 00:06:56,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's a good answer.

96
00:06:57,293 --> 00:06:59,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where is that suffering that I want to be free from?

97
00:07:01,015 --> 00:07:02,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that suffering in the future?

98
00:07:02,636 --> 00:07:03,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll practice dhamma in the future.

99
00:07:04,537 --> 00:07:06,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is that liability to suffering in the present?

100
00:07:06,858 --> 00:07:07,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's in the present.

101
00:07:08,039 --> 00:07:10,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does that liability to suffering in the present manifest to me, really?

102
00:07:11,001 --> 00:07:17,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, through me experiencing unwanted things through my own actions.

103
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:19,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

104
00:07:19,988 --> 00:07:30,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to practice dhamma in the future, when you get your conditions ready, while in reality, all you need to do is start recognizing where your motivation is rooted, and you'll be practicing dhamma for the same purpose of being free from suffering.

105
00:07:32,499 --> 00:07:39,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that... Can you suffer in any way, if you don't want anything?

106
00:07:40,226 --> 00:07:43,189
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't want to have anything, and you don't want to keep anything.

107
00:07:43,687 --> 00:07:43,867
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

108
00:07:44,147 --> 00:07:47,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't care about losing anything, or you don't care about gaining anything.

109
00:07:47,289 --> 00:07:50,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you experience any discrepancy that would result in suffering?

110
00:07:50,490 --> 00:07:54,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you then suffer if you don't have any desire whatsoever?

111
00:07:55,713 --> 00:07:57,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Any emotional investment whatsoever?

112
00:07:59,015 --> 00:08:04,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, whenever you do suffer, it's because you're emotionally invested.

113
00:08:04,417 --> 00:08:04,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

114
00:08:04,958 --> 00:08:09,400
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, whenever you're emotionally invested, means you act out of that as your reason.

115
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:09,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

116
00:08:11,403 --> 00:08:12,083
[SPEAKER_01]: More pleasure.

117
00:08:12,243 --> 00:08:13,004
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want pain.

118
00:08:13,444 --> 00:08:14,404
[SPEAKER_01]: I want a distraction.

119
00:08:14,564 --> 00:08:15,325
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want...

120
00:08:15,405 --> 00:08:17,326
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want like a nothingness, neutral feeling.

121
00:08:17,526 --> 00:08:20,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, you're acting out of that desire.

122
00:08:22,308 --> 00:08:25,269
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just simply keeping the whole thing going.

123
00:08:25,469 --> 00:08:25,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

124
00:08:25,889 --> 00:08:26,670
[SPEAKER_01]: You're sustaining it.

125
00:08:26,690 --> 00:08:27,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You're maintaining it.

126
00:08:27,410 --> 00:08:28,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You're feeding it.

127
00:08:29,311 --> 00:08:37,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the transparency... and again, can you develop transparency in regard to your motivation, if you don't even restrain your actions?

128
00:08:38,795 --> 00:08:39,015
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

129
00:08:40,235 --> 00:08:50,362
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're physically, sort of, already fully rooted in desire and acting towards more desire... Yeah, because those actions are still, you know, that you're keeping... Well, exactly, exactly.

130
00:08:50,382 --> 00:08:51,262
[SPEAKER_01]: They're fully rooted in that.

131
00:08:51,302 --> 00:08:53,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter how much you might try to mentally resist.

132
00:08:53,844 --> 00:08:54,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

133
00:08:54,345 --> 00:08:59,608
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you're bodily engaging with with things that are rooted in desire means you you are fully within that cycle.

134
00:08:59,768 --> 00:09:05,352
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess, I guess, like, that's the things people... the practice isn't some kind of, like,

135
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:08,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that's separate from your life.

136
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:17,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, oh, I just live my life like this and then, all right, now I'll do some practice that's completely got nothing to do with... Basically, if you do practice rightly, it will undermine your life.

137
00:09:18,103 --> 00:09:19,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It will start undoing your life.

138
00:09:20,023 --> 00:09:22,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing your very bhāva, your very being.

139
00:09:22,084 --> 00:09:28,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your life, your life is everything that you're doing, which is determined by your desire.

140
00:09:28,105 --> 00:09:32,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha speaks about the truth and seeing the truth and so on, it's not the truth, you know, a secret.

141
00:09:34,274 --> 00:09:39,021
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the truth in regard to your own experience, where my actions are rooted.

142
00:09:39,902 --> 00:09:47,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can actually recognize what you said earlier on, that there are certain things that are unknown, cannot be known.

143
00:09:47,959 --> 00:09:51,483
[SPEAKER_01]: cannot be empirically, sort of, verified in this life.

144
00:09:52,203 --> 00:09:52,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a fact.

145
00:09:53,144 --> 00:09:55,547
[SPEAKER_01]: That is... Or even, it can be, but not right now.

146
00:09:55,847 --> 00:09:56,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

147
00:09:56,588 --> 00:10:02,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, right now, what is empirically verifiable is that these things cannot be empirically verified.

148
00:10:02,254 --> 00:10:02,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

149
00:10:03,235 --> 00:10:04,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And you recognize that.

150
00:10:06,198 --> 00:10:10,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize... and the way... the reason why you can recognize that is because you realize

151
00:10:11,219 --> 00:10:13,841
[SPEAKER_01]: It was never the point of figuring these things out.

152
00:10:14,282 --> 00:10:22,549
[SPEAKER_01]: The point was simply to not maintain greed, aversion, delusion, not maintain desire on the level of body action, speech action, mental action.

153
00:10:22,609 --> 00:10:23,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

154
00:10:23,650 --> 00:10:31,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't have a problem with not knowing things, or haven't figured them long enough, because you you stop assuming that the answer is at the end of those things.

155
00:10:32,237 --> 00:10:36,581
[SPEAKER_00]: And you start to see that, actually, your attempts to try to find out about these things...

156
00:10:36,621 --> 00:10:37,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

157
00:10:37,181 --> 00:10:41,124
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already rooted in something, and if you don't ignore that, that's the lacking of truth.

158
00:10:41,625 --> 00:10:48,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in, like, lacking of experiencing, like, certain content or certain answers of your experience.

159
00:10:48,130 --> 00:10:53,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, even when people do a meditation technique,

160
00:10:54,548 --> 00:10:58,489
[SPEAKER_01]: That obviously will involve this kind of time and then result of some special experience.

161
00:10:58,509 --> 00:10:58,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

162
00:10:58,929 --> 00:11:01,030
[SPEAKER_01]: They remain ignorant of their motivation throughout.

163
00:11:01,330 --> 00:11:01,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

164
00:11:01,630 --> 00:11:03,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you become aware, you don't need a technique.

165
00:11:03,951 --> 00:11:04,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

166
00:11:04,491 --> 00:11:06,572
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the field where any effort applies.

167
00:11:07,892 --> 00:11:12,574
[SPEAKER_01]: The field of motivation behind me wanted to go retreat, to do this technique, to do that technique.

168
00:11:12,934 --> 00:11:13,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

169
00:11:13,954 --> 00:11:15,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I want to be free from suffering.

170
00:11:15,255 --> 00:11:16,535
[SPEAKER_01]: What is free from suffering for me?

171
00:11:17,236 --> 00:11:21,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it a special... Do I assume it's some sort of experience whereby everything else will be cancelled by it?

172
00:11:23,352 --> 00:11:24,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it something else, perhaps?

173
00:11:24,793 --> 00:11:26,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I investigated long enough to see?

174
00:11:26,614 --> 00:11:31,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... the more... the clearer you are for... about your motives, the clearer you will become about what your goal is as well.

175
00:11:33,939 --> 00:11:41,824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha talked about the mundane right view, which is recognizing there is good action, there is bad action.

176
00:11:42,144 --> 00:11:44,586
[SPEAKER_01]: That literally means, ah, so there's a difference.

177
00:11:45,106 --> 00:11:47,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Like it... by the way, the action can be same externally.

178
00:11:48,237 --> 00:11:48,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

179
00:11:48,778 --> 00:11:56,425
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a difference whether that action is rooted in absence of desire, absence of greed, aversion, delusion, or is it rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

180
00:11:56,865 --> 00:12:00,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You do the same thing externally, it's two different actions.

181
00:12:00,248 --> 00:12:08,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stop judging action by the content, you start seeing it by the motivation behind, which are its necessary, presently enduring roots.

182
00:12:09,216 --> 00:12:09,997
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

183
00:12:10,177 --> 00:12:13,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was always struck by the fact that

184
00:12:15,175 --> 00:12:19,058
[SPEAKER_00]: The common sense tells you that you've got an action.

185
00:12:19,078 --> 00:12:21,941
[SPEAKER_00]: We say you're talking about, like, observing an action externally.

186
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:26,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that person is walking over there, and that person is also walking over there.

187
00:12:26,365 --> 00:12:27,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're doing the same thing.

188
00:12:27,626 --> 00:12:28,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Same action, yeah.

189
00:12:28,467 --> 00:12:29,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Same action.

190
00:12:29,047 --> 00:12:29,487
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not...

191
00:12:29,528 --> 00:12:31,049
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're doing it for different reasons.

192
00:12:31,089 --> 00:12:34,492
[SPEAKER_00]: He's going over there to get away from something, and he's going over there because he wants something, or whatever.

193
00:12:36,297 --> 00:12:42,801
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you distinguish them between the act, the action, the external action, and the motivation.

194
00:12:42,861 --> 00:12:45,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And what you're saying is motivation is more important.

195
00:12:45,302 --> 00:12:47,704
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, the Buddha says the action is the motivation.

196
00:12:48,704 --> 00:12:49,585
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not two different things.

197
00:12:50,525 --> 00:12:57,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, to talk about the external act is kind of... Well, that's not even an action.

198
00:12:57,289 --> 00:12:57,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

199
00:12:58,010 --> 00:13:01,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why he says cetanā is... Where the action is.

200
00:13:01,692 --> 00:13:01,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Action.

201
00:13:02,112 --> 00:13:02,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Khamma.

202
00:13:02,432 --> 00:13:03,092
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's what I'm saying.

203
00:13:03,112 --> 00:13:03,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not...

204
00:13:05,771 --> 00:13:08,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what you described is the common sense.

205
00:13:08,592 --> 00:13:09,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, common sense.

206
00:13:09,173 --> 00:13:10,253
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what action is.

207
00:13:10,373 --> 00:13:11,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

208
00:13:11,834 --> 00:13:16,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so... Yeah, every action is intention.

209
00:13:16,636 --> 00:13:16,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

210
00:13:16,996 --> 00:13:23,619
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your intention is your action, even if you don't make a physically discernible, visible by others action.

211
00:13:23,739 --> 00:13:23,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

212
00:13:24,339 --> 00:13:26,500
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already committed to that action through your intention.

213
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:29,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everyone's measure of this external action.

214
00:13:29,622 --> 00:13:31,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is what he does and we can observe externally.

215
00:13:31,583 --> 00:13:32,163
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what action is.

216
00:13:32,183 --> 00:13:33,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It's measuring the results of action.

217
00:13:33,684 --> 00:13:34,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

218
00:13:34,824 --> 00:13:46,333
[SPEAKER_01]: It's measuring that which is basically rooted in that which is... which is... if you think that's what action is, you're not going to see what action really is, which is the intention that binds it all, like, throughout, so to speak.

219
00:13:47,574 --> 00:13:56,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Think of the intention as the container of all the acts by body, speech and mind you do within that context of intended, resulting consequence.

220
00:13:58,142 --> 00:14:03,467
[SPEAKER_00]: The example... there's an example that Venerable Jnanavira came up with in his early writings was

221
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:11,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone's drinking a cup of tea and they put a spoonful of sugar into their tea.

222
00:14:12,782 --> 00:14:16,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're watching this and you know that that's arsenic.

223
00:14:17,004 --> 00:14:18,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're thinking, why is he killing himself?

224
00:14:20,513 --> 00:14:26,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... your measure for what it is, this external... what's going on out there... Yeah.

225
00:14:26,317 --> 00:14:27,197
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the act.

226
00:14:27,298 --> 00:14:28,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he doesn't know it's arsonic.

227
00:14:28,558 --> 00:14:36,243
[SPEAKER_01]: And if people are... when people are not aware of their own motivation, then they start assuming motivation of others simply based on the observing of the external acts.

228
00:14:36,263 --> 00:14:36,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

229
00:14:36,904 --> 00:14:38,165
[SPEAKER_01]: And we spoke about it in other talks.

230
00:14:38,205 --> 00:14:38,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

231
00:14:38,325 --> 00:14:41,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Where somebody would have unacceptable speech.

232
00:14:41,327 --> 00:14:41,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

233
00:14:41,547 --> 00:14:45,410
[SPEAKER_01]: People just assume ill will and anger and conceit, yet he's an arahant.

234
00:14:45,790 --> 00:14:46,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

235
00:14:47,702 --> 00:14:57,665
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be much more, kind of, prolific in the extent of that, assuming other people's motivation, the more you're unaware of your own.

236
00:14:58,565 --> 00:15:03,546
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you become aware of your own, well, the more accurate in reading other becomes, without needing to assume it.

237
00:15:04,626 --> 00:15:08,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, you know, you're becoming familiar with how motivation works, really.

238
00:15:08,887 --> 00:15:09,448
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

239
00:15:09,468 --> 00:15:11,068
[SPEAKER_01]: The action is not what you've done.

240
00:15:11,388 --> 00:15:14,409
[SPEAKER_01]: The action is the intention within which is something you've done.

241
00:15:15,860 --> 00:15:18,381
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, that's why it's a mundane right view.

242
00:15:19,061 --> 00:15:21,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it's already aligning itself with what the right view is.

243
00:15:22,963 --> 00:15:26,944
[SPEAKER_01]: You start recognizing where the work is, where the importance is.

244
00:15:28,185 --> 00:15:33,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You start recognizing the criteria for what is wholesome and for what is unwholesome.

245
00:15:33,387 --> 00:15:36,248
[SPEAKER_01]: And that criteria for people is external, when they start.

246
00:15:36,328 --> 00:15:38,609
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all, you shouldn't do that, you must do this.

247
00:15:39,069 --> 00:15:39,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter why.

248
00:15:41,153 --> 00:15:45,755
[SPEAKER_01]: You just must adhere to this form, to this method, to this technique, and then it's right.

249
00:15:45,895 --> 00:15:46,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

250
00:15:46,235 --> 00:15:46,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

251
00:15:46,975 --> 00:15:52,317
[SPEAKER_01]: You can adhere to the technique the rest of your life for completely wrong reasons, and that's why you will not even have a mundane right view.

252
00:15:52,337 --> 00:15:52,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

253
00:15:52,637 --> 00:15:52,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:15:53,218 --> 00:15:54,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone be free from suffering.

255
00:15:55,338 --> 00:15:55,919
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

256
00:15:55,979 --> 00:15:58,439
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the important thing is this mundane right view.

257
00:15:58,499 --> 00:15:59,380
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's mundane.

258
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:00,840
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's... it's...

259
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:02,161
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mundane, like, common.

260
00:16:03,158 --> 00:16:03,918
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but it's but...

261
00:16:03,938 --> 00:16:06,939
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like it doesn't pertain to that universal theorem from suffering.

262
00:16:06,979 --> 00:16:09,699
[SPEAKER_00]: You could say there are even... there are people who have no interest in...

263
00:16:10,099 --> 00:16:12,460
[SPEAKER_00]: In dhamma and are closer to the mundane right view.

264
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:12,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

265
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:15,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are straightforward in their, you know, thinking.

266
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:15,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Transparency.

267
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:15,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

268
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:20,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Because their thinking does not violate the basic principles of that, like, universal nature of things.

269
00:16:20,941 --> 00:16:21,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

270
00:16:21,401 --> 00:16:22,341
[SPEAKER_00]: They're looking at what they're doing.

271
00:16:22,361 --> 00:16:23,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing this?

272
00:16:23,041 --> 00:16:23,502
[SPEAKER_00]: What's this?

273
00:16:23,562 --> 00:16:28,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole... the whole thing, the whole thing of that description of the mundane right view, like, there is given, there's

274
00:16:32,103 --> 00:16:37,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in what you observe, but things are rooted in, basically, motivation behind it.

275
00:16:37,989 --> 00:16:51,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, as I said many times before, certain things, externally done, always have to be rooted in certain intentions, such as killing, stealing, cheating, whatever, sexual intercourse from the point of view of a celibate person, and so on.

276
00:16:52,243 --> 00:16:57,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon that, but everything else is determined by the intention behind, not by the content of what you're doing.

277
00:16:57,907 --> 00:17:04,531
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, there is mother, there is father, there is good, there is bad, there is responsibility for my own actions.

278
00:17:05,412 --> 00:17:11,215
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, oh, responsibility for me walking, sitting, talking, doing this, is on me.

279
00:17:12,036 --> 00:17:16,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on the reasoning that I have towards the world, so to speak.

280
00:17:16,258 --> 00:17:21,342
[SPEAKER_01]: When people say, oh, well, no, I went there because such and such said so, that I should do it.

281
00:17:22,015 --> 00:17:22,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

282
00:17:22,576 --> 00:17:34,088
[SPEAKER_01]: You might do that, you might choose for that to be the reason for your action, but you will not be able to ignore that you are responsible for choosing that such and such person said something and that was your reason for going.

283
00:17:34,108 --> 00:17:35,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can always say no to it.

284
00:17:35,530 --> 00:17:36,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

285
00:17:38,673 --> 00:17:43,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so the the the usual state of affairs is someone

286
00:17:44,598 --> 00:17:46,279
[SPEAKER_00]: I could always say no to that.

287
00:17:46,719 --> 00:17:48,460
[SPEAKER_00]: I could always say no to the group.

288
00:18:07,967 --> 00:18:08,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

289
00:18:08,327 --> 00:18:12,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is not to, like, go with the group or against the group.

290
00:18:12,230 --> 00:18:14,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you should always say no to the group.

291
00:18:14,071 --> 00:18:17,593
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to recognize that you're responsible for whatever you choose to do.

292
00:18:18,194 --> 00:18:19,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I say yes or no, it's my choice.

293
00:18:20,555 --> 00:18:23,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cannot abstain from choosing.

294
00:18:24,097 --> 00:18:26,699
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then abstain you from responsibility for your choice.

295
00:18:27,139 --> 00:18:29,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Because saying, I will not choose, is your choice.

296
00:18:30,381 --> 00:18:37,006
[SPEAKER_01]: My choice is to abstain from choice A and choice B. Oh, so you're responsible for abstaining from choice A and choice B. Yeah.

297
00:18:37,546 --> 00:18:42,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would... why do people then... Avoid responsibility for your choice, because it's unpleasant.

298
00:18:42,569 --> 00:18:42,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

299
00:18:43,890 --> 00:18:47,552
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels a little bit... primordial perfect form of dukkha.

300
00:18:47,632 --> 00:18:47,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

301
00:18:48,212 --> 00:18:53,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha literally means opposite of sukha, means dis-ease or unease.

302
00:18:53,255 --> 00:18:56,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, you know, suffering and despair of the world.

303
00:18:56,697 --> 00:18:56,797
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

304
00:18:56,857 --> 00:19:00,139
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that uneasiness in regard to everything.

305
00:19:00,319 --> 00:19:00,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

306
00:19:00,920 --> 00:19:04,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, literally, everything that you experience

307
00:19:06,723 --> 00:19:12,085
[SPEAKER_01]: is of that universal nature of it's not in my control and that's why it makes me uneasy.

308
00:19:15,866 --> 00:19:17,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can... you have two ways of going about it.

309
00:19:18,067 --> 00:19:23,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Either pretend it's not there, ignore it, which you can only do as a starting point, so to speak.

310
00:19:24,149 --> 00:19:31,052
[SPEAKER_01]: We all start by ignoring the dukkha, ignoring the universal truth of that nature, which then obviously results in everything else.

311
00:19:32,736 --> 00:19:36,780
[SPEAKER_01]: The self-view, craving, desire, lust, that then fuels it even more.

312
00:19:37,020 --> 00:19:40,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can we say, like, so Sartre would have called that bad faith?

313
00:19:41,843 --> 00:19:43,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, it's a form of faith.

314
00:19:43,825 --> 00:19:47,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, literally, you have thing right in front of your face and you pretend it's not there.

315
00:19:48,248 --> 00:19:50,890
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that this, that this, it's not there.

316
00:19:51,071 --> 00:19:54,333
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already your existence, it's already a contradiction in terms.

317
00:19:54,433 --> 00:19:57,115
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that these things, they were given to me.

318
00:19:57,215 --> 00:20:00,358
[SPEAKER_01]: My feelings have a reason for me, perceptions have a reason for me.

319
00:20:01,412 --> 00:20:05,453
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe I have created them by being their master and controller.

320
00:20:05,533 --> 00:20:06,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you have no say in them.

321
00:20:07,533 --> 00:20:13,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still, while not having say in these things you feel, you still maintain the notion that you are the owner of what you feel.

322
00:20:18,415 --> 00:20:22,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you ignore everything else that would contradict that ownership.

323
00:20:22,876 --> 00:20:25,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why dukkha cannot enter you.

324
00:20:26,797 --> 00:20:30,598
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot be liable to it, exposed to it, unless you make yourself so.

325
00:20:32,098 --> 00:20:43,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through ignoring what's obviously there, lack of control, lack of safety, lack of easiness, that's why you become subjected to lack of safety.

326
00:20:44,184 --> 00:20:47,426
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you become unsafe and uneasy and so on.

327
00:20:50,688 --> 00:20:54,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason you ignore it is because it's unpleasant.

328
00:20:55,611 --> 00:20:58,152
[SPEAKER_01]: But because of that, you experience even more unpleasure.

329
00:20:58,392 --> 00:20:58,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

330
00:20:59,773 --> 00:21:01,674
[SPEAKER_01]: This great mass of suffering, as the Buddha would say.

331
00:21:02,523 --> 00:21:06,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, oh, so you have no choice about this pleasure.

332
00:21:07,045 --> 00:21:08,606
[SPEAKER_01]: You have only choice about how much of it.

333
00:21:10,007 --> 00:21:23,494
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, well, if I actually accept this, which now seems worse, this pleasure of anxiety and uneasiness and lack of control, and endure it, I'm actually overcoming all the other suffering that would be as a result of avoiding this.

334
00:21:25,203 --> 00:21:34,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, even as a mundane right view of a puthujjana, you would be doing... already undoing a lot of... or already removing a lot of dust from your eyes.

335
00:21:34,969 --> 00:21:38,451
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of wrong views and other dependencies on greed, aversion and delusion.

336
00:21:42,213 --> 00:21:43,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering is right there.

337
00:21:44,555 --> 00:21:46,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability to suffering is right there.

338
00:21:47,216 --> 00:21:54,461
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, would you be able to experience any form of suffering, any form of pain, any form of discomfort,

339
00:21:55,453 --> 00:22:01,435
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have a desire for anything you gained or anything you lost...

340
00:22:02,495 --> 00:22:12,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if the only reason you're giving to desire is to be free from pain, yet desire is the reason for any form of pain, why don't you just abandon the desire?

341
00:22:13,018 --> 00:22:14,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus you abandon the pain.

342
00:22:14,918 --> 00:22:16,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is your goal all along.

343
00:22:17,399 --> 00:22:18,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Right from the start.

344
00:22:19,423 --> 00:22:28,471
[SPEAKER_01]: The worst asottava puthujjana, without any inclination to practice the dhamma, and the best, the purest, the greatest of the arahants, same motivation.

345
00:22:28,811 --> 00:22:29,752
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from pain.

346
00:22:30,092 --> 00:22:32,134
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just how they go about it.

347
00:22:35,296 --> 00:22:38,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to avoid it, or not appropriating it.

348
00:22:38,639 --> 00:22:41,902
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the transparency we talk about, then, is about undoing.

349
00:22:42,558 --> 00:22:46,782
[SPEAKER_01]: These contradictions that you carry already within yourself, they are making you liable to suffering.

350
00:22:47,242 --> 00:22:51,325
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about figuring out the truth at the end of your meditation retreat.

351
00:22:51,345 --> 00:22:55,249
[SPEAKER_01]: They would then result in cancellation of all the contradictions and the problems that make you suffer.

352
00:22:56,980 --> 00:22:57,401
[SPEAKER_00]: There are a lot...

353
00:22:57,421 --> 00:23:07,052
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there are lots... there are kind of more... there are grosser types of bad faith that people will... Well, bad faith is as endless as a vijay is.

354
00:23:07,072 --> 00:23:07,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

355
00:23:07,493 --> 00:23:08,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what a vijay is.

356
00:23:09,655 --> 00:23:11,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, someone might...

357
00:23:11,217 --> 00:23:12,459
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to ignore something?

358
00:23:13,032 --> 00:23:17,154
[SPEAKER_01]: To turn a blind eye to it, you need to know that there is at least that thing there.

359
00:23:17,334 --> 00:23:17,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

360
00:23:18,115 --> 00:23:19,976
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot ignore that which does not exist.

361
00:23:20,016 --> 00:23:20,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

362
00:23:20,676 --> 00:23:21,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the weird thing.

363
00:23:21,356 --> 00:23:22,617
[SPEAKER_00]: You're kind of lying to yourself.

364
00:23:22,637 --> 00:23:25,238
[SPEAKER_00]: But how can you lie to yourself because you know... Yeah.

365
00:23:25,319 --> 00:23:27,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that's exactly how... that's why people suffer.

366
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:27,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

367
00:23:27,680 --> 00:23:30,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people would suffer on account of what other people say or do.

368
00:23:30,141 --> 00:23:30,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

369
00:23:30,301 --> 00:23:33,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it would remind them of the stuff they're trying to cover up for themselves.

370
00:23:34,363 --> 00:23:39,506
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're free from any form of existential cover-up, there is nothing that can provoke you or engage you or affect you.

371
00:23:43,770 --> 00:23:48,234
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't crave safety, how could you possibly suffer on account of lack of safety?

372
00:23:49,075 --> 00:23:55,520
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't crave control, if you don't imply control, if you don't assume control, how can you suffer on account of random things happening to you?

373
00:23:57,375 --> 00:24:00,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by the way, when I say control, I don't mean controlling the world or the things.

374
00:24:00,236 --> 00:24:02,977
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, people assume control over their own feelings.

375
00:24:02,997 --> 00:24:03,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

376
00:24:03,217 --> 00:24:07,878
[SPEAKER_01]: While, at the same time, simultaneously, they know... they have no say in those feelings arising.

377
00:24:07,898 --> 00:24:14,440
[SPEAKER_01]: They know they're subjected to it, but even that recognition of unsubjected feeling, they put within their assumption of control.

378
00:24:14,480 --> 00:24:14,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:24:16,040 --> 00:24:16,760
[SPEAKER_00]: They get the order.

380
00:24:17,401 --> 00:24:17,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

381
00:24:19,711 --> 00:24:22,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how often the Buddha would ask people who would have approached him like that.

382
00:24:23,134 --> 00:24:24,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so you are in control of...

383
00:24:24,335 --> 00:24:25,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Which feeling is then in your control?

384
00:24:26,597 --> 00:24:27,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it pleasant?

385
00:24:27,817 --> 00:24:28,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it unpleasant?

386
00:24:28,698 --> 00:24:29,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the neutral one?

387
00:24:30,159 --> 00:24:34,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there always one of them can only be present, not all of them.

388
00:24:35,283 --> 00:24:39,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are in the control of pleasant feeling, how come then it changes into unpleasant one?

389
00:24:40,728 --> 00:24:43,370
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're in control of unpleasant, how come it changes into neutral?

390
00:24:49,290 --> 00:24:49,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's it.

391
00:24:49,751 --> 00:24:54,476
[SPEAKER_00]: When you sort of... you can be reminded that, yeah, no, actually, I'm not in control of these feelings.

392
00:24:54,816 --> 00:24:55,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

393
00:24:55,798 --> 00:24:59,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why people avoid such reminders is because they're unpleasant.

394
00:25:00,042 --> 00:25:00,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

395
00:25:00,483 --> 00:25:04,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're trying to outrun discomfort of certain recognitions within yourself.

396
00:25:05,529 --> 00:25:07,091
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically you trying to control your feelings.

397
00:25:07,972 --> 00:25:08,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

398
00:25:09,414 --> 00:25:13,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you are in a position to outrun that which you're subjected to.

399
00:25:13,456 --> 00:25:13,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

400
00:25:13,976 --> 00:25:14,817
[SPEAKER_01]: You're ignoring it.

401
00:25:15,097 --> 00:25:16,338
[SPEAKER_01]: You're turning a blind eye to it.

402
00:25:16,378 --> 00:25:17,338
[SPEAKER_01]: You're pretending it's not there.

403
00:25:17,578 --> 00:25:20,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to cover up with a set of feelings you want.

404
00:25:21,641 --> 00:25:23,522
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're kind of living a contradiction.

405
00:25:24,102 --> 00:25:24,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

406
00:25:24,522 --> 00:25:25,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what bhāva is.

407
00:25:25,543 --> 00:25:26,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhāva is a contradiction.

408
00:25:26,583 --> 00:25:26,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

409
00:25:27,103 --> 00:25:29,064
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the contradiction, the existence is ceased.

410
00:25:29,505 --> 00:25:31,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant is contradiction-free.

411
00:25:31,186 --> 00:25:31,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

412
00:25:31,806 --> 00:25:35,488
[SPEAKER_00]: The transparency begins by acknowledging

413
00:25:36,977 --> 00:25:41,580
[SPEAKER_00]: The contradictions, you know, that, and like you said... By rounding them up, basically, it's a subtler, subtler.

414
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

415
00:25:43,081 --> 00:25:45,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but for most people, I mean, it's just a...

416
00:25:45,503 --> 00:25:53,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize, what's, what's, what's the first, what would be the most common contradiction of, of, say, a lay person wants to start practicing the dhamma?

417
00:25:54,309 --> 00:25:55,970
[SPEAKER_01]: The contradiction they have to start saying no to.

418
00:25:58,111 --> 00:25:59,973
[SPEAKER_00]: The contradiction they have to start saying no to.

419
00:26:00,033 --> 00:26:02,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, to start rounding it up, start abstaining from.

420
00:26:03,435 --> 00:26:03,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Precepts.

421
00:26:04,316 --> 00:26:04,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

422
00:26:05,855 --> 00:26:12,460
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here you are, wanting to develop freedom from suffering, on account of practicing from the dhamma.

423
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:12,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

424
00:26:13,581 --> 00:26:15,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And... And I'm still doing this.

425
00:26:16,503 --> 00:26:16,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

426
00:26:16,763 --> 00:26:19,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to develop, basically, the strength of mind... Yeah.

427
00:26:19,546 --> 00:26:22,048
[SPEAKER_01]: ...that will be immovable... Yeah.

428
00:26:22,068 --> 00:26:25,290
[SPEAKER_01]: ...in regard to anything that I might be subjected to.

429
00:26:27,031 --> 00:26:30,194
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in the meantime, I will keep acting out of my weakness... Yeah.

430
00:26:30,214 --> 00:26:31,655
[SPEAKER_01]: ...in order to develop strength.

431
00:26:32,195 --> 00:26:32,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

432
00:26:32,476 --> 00:26:33,737
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a contradiction in terms.

433
00:26:33,877 --> 00:26:34,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

434
00:26:34,590 --> 00:26:45,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you want to heal your itchy skin, infected, whatever, ulcers that are really itchy, you have to stop, start scratching and making it worse.

435
00:26:45,895 --> 00:26:46,896
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

436
00:26:47,916 --> 00:26:54,359
[SPEAKER_01]: You still don't know necessarily what the actual cure might be, but you'd already know you should stop making it worse.

437
00:26:55,219 --> 00:27:02,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Because one thing for sure, even if you find the cure later on, it won't work because you've destroyed your whole arm with your scratching.

438
00:27:04,948 --> 00:27:12,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you already know that certain types of behavior are rooted in being unable to control yourself, unable to restrain yourself.

439
00:27:12,633 --> 00:27:22,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are, practicing for the ultimate immovability, the ultimate kind of restraint in regard to giving in to pain, giving in to pleasure, giving in to anything that moves.

440
00:27:23,320 --> 00:27:26,683
[SPEAKER_01]: You want peace on account of which you keep disturbing yourself.

441
00:27:26,843 --> 00:27:27,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

442
00:27:27,923 --> 00:27:34,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You basically want to... You're looking for freedom from ill-will and sense desire.

443
00:27:35,762 --> 00:27:39,203
[SPEAKER_00]: You give in to your will and sense desire if you want to be free from it.

444
00:27:39,243 --> 00:27:40,663
[SPEAKER_01]: You're acting out of that.

445
00:27:41,323 --> 00:27:41,843
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

446
00:27:42,163 --> 00:27:54,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if both central craving, desire and ill-will were not originally and fundamentally unpleasant, you wouldn't be feeling pressured to deal with it.

447
00:27:55,906 --> 00:27:56,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I said.

448
00:27:57,146 --> 00:28:04,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Those acts, those experiences are always underlined by that craving.

449
00:28:05,863 --> 00:28:07,124
[SPEAKER_01]: By more of that craving.

450
00:28:10,427 --> 00:28:12,369
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you know that you are precious.

451
00:28:12,509 --> 00:28:12,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

452
00:28:12,889 --> 00:28:22,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And so... Like, you might crave for pleasure, but the only reason you crave for that sensual pleasure is because, simultaneously, underneath it, you crave for freedom from the current.

453
00:28:22,378 --> 00:28:22,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

454
00:28:23,199 --> 00:28:23,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

455
00:28:23,539 --> 00:28:28,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the context of me is, I don't want this pain of not having that, thus I'll crave for that.

456
00:28:28,564 --> 00:28:28,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

457
00:28:30,271 --> 00:28:34,778
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... what you're craving towards it, it's underlined by even more craving.

458
00:28:35,059 --> 00:28:38,063
[SPEAKER_01]: They're both simultaneously there, and one feeds the other.

459
00:28:38,744 --> 00:28:38,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

460
00:28:41,118 --> 00:28:44,641
[SPEAKER_00]: But looking at this thing that you're heading towards allows you to ignore...

461
00:28:44,681 --> 00:28:45,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

462
00:28:45,502 --> 00:28:50,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And allows you, like, that hope that that will be the escape of this current pain.

463
00:28:50,666 --> 00:28:51,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

464
00:28:51,287 --> 00:28:53,529
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sensuality is on the level of assumption.

465
00:28:53,549 --> 00:28:54,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the gratification.

466
00:28:55,070 --> 00:28:55,931
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the gratification.

467
00:28:55,991 --> 00:28:58,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Not getting it, but hoping that getting it will free you from it.

468
00:28:59,795 --> 00:29:02,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why sensuality is the assumption, as I said.

469
00:29:02,237 --> 00:29:02,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

470
00:29:03,130 --> 00:29:07,192
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... Deriving pleasure from sense objects is not what sensuality is.

471
00:29:08,072 --> 00:29:13,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's assuming that pleasure, simultaneously craving for avoiding the pain of not having it.

472
00:29:13,635 --> 00:29:15,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's on the level of a view.

473
00:29:17,356 --> 00:29:24,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first contradiction a layperson has to undo is the contradiction of lack of restraint.

474
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:26,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Contradiction of not keeping the precepts.

475
00:29:26,861 --> 00:29:29,582
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a contradiction to your own goal of wanting to practice the dhamma.

476
00:29:31,302 --> 00:29:38,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, ask yourself, like, even if you're not sure... Well, first, you have to be... you have to read the suttas and become familiar with what the actual goal of dhamma is.

477
00:29:39,126 --> 00:29:45,610
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, even you're somewhere in between, you're unclear, ask yourself what enlightenment would... what would enlightenment entail for me?

478
00:29:45,630 --> 00:29:46,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's it.

479
00:29:46,150 --> 00:29:46,911
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was thinking.

480
00:29:46,971 --> 00:29:58,557
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, there's so many contradictions you can think of... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that... that...

481
00:30:00,839 --> 00:30:01,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining contradiction.

482
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:05,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to make the effort to, oh, I'll keep this contradiction.

483
00:30:05,543 --> 00:30:07,725
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is ignore it, and that's how you're keeping it.

484
00:30:08,505 --> 00:30:13,209
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'd say it's quite common, the idea of...

485
00:30:13,229 --> 00:30:21,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I want to practice for enlightenment, and yet enlightenment is this thing that will happen to me... Out of my control.

486
00:30:21,255 --> 00:30:21,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of my control.

487
00:30:22,336 --> 00:30:24,518
[SPEAKER_01]: If I do these things rightly...

488
00:30:24,538 --> 00:30:29,162
[SPEAKER_01]: If I do this thing here, then something other will suddenly happen to me.

489
00:30:30,472 --> 00:30:32,641
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already the first thing you should start questioning.

490
00:30:32,722 --> 00:30:32,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

491
00:30:33,746 --> 00:30:33,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Well...

492
00:30:34,775 --> 00:30:40,678
[SPEAKER_01]: You might want to believe that that's how enlightenment works, but then, if you read the suttas, you realize that's not what the Buddha said.

493
00:30:41,158 --> 00:30:42,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they don't read the suttas.

494
00:30:42,538 --> 00:30:45,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, most people probably don't read the suttas.

495
00:30:45,960 --> 00:30:47,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I want to practice as the...

496
00:30:47,801 --> 00:30:48,141
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to...

497
00:30:48,161 --> 00:30:50,982
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the Buddha's probably onto something, but I won't find out.

498
00:30:51,482 --> 00:30:52,023
[SPEAKER_01]: I won't find out.

499
00:30:52,223 --> 00:30:53,163
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another contradiction.

500
00:30:53,283 --> 00:30:58,726
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to practice the Buddha's teaching, but I also don't want to make the effort to find out what that teaching clearly is.

501
00:30:59,126 --> 00:31:01,867
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just hope that these other people that talk about...

502
00:31:01,907 --> 00:31:02,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that

503
00:31:05,271 --> 00:31:08,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will also prevent you from being clear about your own motives.

504
00:31:08,654 --> 00:31:11,777
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start investigating your motives, as I said, your goal will become clearer.

505
00:31:12,277 --> 00:31:18,483
[SPEAKER_01]: If your goal of practicing dhamma is clearer, then it will make obvious sense to invest effort in finding out what that dhamma is.

506
00:31:20,196 --> 00:31:21,637
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you're not there...

507
00:31:21,697 --> 00:31:26,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though it's quite a difficult thing to do, there's so many books there, so many things to read, but... Start.

508
00:31:27,122 --> 00:31:27,882
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to start somewhere.

509
00:31:27,902 --> 00:31:28,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that shouldn't stop me.

510
00:31:28,883 --> 00:31:29,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

511
00:31:29,524 --> 00:31:32,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Means like, oh, yeah, okay, so that's the extent of the work that needs to be done.

512
00:31:32,506 --> 00:31:34,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, it's too much of it, thus I won't do any of it.

513
00:31:34,468 --> 00:31:35,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

514
00:31:35,209 --> 00:31:38,491
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... that's just what's... And that's what I've said many times.

515
00:31:38,731 --> 00:31:43,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, a person who recognizes what's for his own welfare, as in, whether I engage with

516
00:31:47,492 --> 00:31:50,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Which suffering has greater prospect of resulting in cessation of suffering?

517
00:31:51,213 --> 00:31:54,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging with sensuality and prolonging desire, or abstaining from it?

518
00:31:54,054 --> 00:31:55,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you already didn't have the direction.

519
00:31:56,595 --> 00:32:01,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, that person would go and find those who teach abstaining from sensuality, to learn more about it.

520
00:32:02,457 --> 00:32:04,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he would ponder on what they said.

521
00:32:04,778 --> 00:32:06,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he would get a sense of it.

522
00:32:06,378 --> 00:32:09,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Having a sense of it, then, he says he measures the task.

523
00:32:09,720 --> 00:32:12,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it becomes clearer to him what needs to be done.

524
00:32:14,241 --> 00:32:16,762
[SPEAKER_01]: When you know what needs to be done, he sets to do it.

525
00:32:17,959 --> 00:32:22,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why people don't know what dhamma practice is, is because they never made the effort to really find out what their task is.

526
00:32:22,941 --> 00:32:23,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

527
00:32:25,101 --> 00:32:29,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, unfortunately, all the effort they made was invested in the wrong direction.

528
00:32:29,923 --> 00:32:30,323
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

529
00:32:30,343 --> 00:32:38,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people hope that just by listening to this talk on YouTube or that book, that will give me all the knowledge I need.

530
00:32:39,626 --> 00:32:39,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

531
00:32:41,526 --> 00:32:44,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ask yourself, what is enlightenment for me?

532
00:32:45,988 --> 00:32:46,708
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I think it is?

533
00:32:47,388 --> 00:32:48,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I want enlightenment.

534
00:32:48,469 --> 00:32:50,389
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I want through that enlightenment?

535
00:32:50,429 --> 00:32:54,110
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the... what is the sort of... what is the goal of it?

536
00:32:54,370 --> 00:32:55,390
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the result of it?

537
00:32:56,351 --> 00:32:57,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it permanent pleasure?

538
00:32:58,851 --> 00:32:59,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

539
00:32:59,311 --> 00:33:00,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Permanent pleasure in what form?

540
00:33:00,572 --> 00:33:03,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Like permanent sensual pleasure or permanent peaceful pleasure?

541
00:33:04,853 --> 00:33:06,373
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess it's permanent peaceful pleasure.

542
00:33:06,773 --> 00:33:09,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the main characteristic of the permanent peace?

543
00:33:10,614 --> 00:33:12,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's absence of disturbances.

544
00:33:13,139 --> 00:33:14,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of what disturbances?

545
00:33:15,020 --> 00:33:22,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, absence of disturbances that I know, and see, and hear, and smell, and taste, and touch, and are unable to be indifferent to words.

546
00:33:24,262 --> 00:33:25,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is more likely then?

547
00:33:25,863 --> 00:33:32,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Would the perfect peace come as a result of you preventing smell, taste, touch, and all the other senses?

548
00:33:33,065 --> 00:33:38,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would perfect peace come as a result of you learning how to be unmoved in regard to smell, sense, and everything else?

549
00:33:39,547 --> 00:33:40,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one makes more sense?

550
00:33:42,894 --> 00:33:44,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, being unmoved makes more sense.

551
00:33:45,416 --> 00:33:52,620
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why don't I start practicing lack of moving in regard to things right here, right now, that move me?

552
00:33:54,422 --> 00:34:00,746
[SPEAKER_01]: It has... Would that have a greater prospect of pertaining to centrality that you already value through your own random efforts?

553
00:34:01,526 --> 00:34:03,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But now it's not so random anymore.

554
00:34:04,068 --> 00:34:11,193
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually well-reasoned, well-contemplated, and you're already getting a sense of direction, which means you're already getting a sense of what needs to be done.

555
00:34:12,205 --> 00:34:15,768
[SPEAKER_00]: And what you're doing there is clearer, more transparent.

556
00:34:15,908 --> 00:34:16,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

557
00:34:16,909 --> 00:34:20,271
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't depend on other people and doesn't depend on the circumstances.

558
00:34:20,691 --> 00:34:22,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in regard to the circumstances.

559
00:34:22,753 --> 00:34:23,573
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

560
00:34:23,974 --> 00:34:24,674
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha said.

561
00:34:24,694 --> 00:34:31,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever is to be obtained by oneself, by one's manly effort, manly strength, manly endurance, I obtain.

562
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:32,600
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm an arahant.

563
00:34:32,660 --> 00:34:33,421
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm fully enlightened.

564
00:34:34,743 --> 00:34:38,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahantship is something you developed, not something that happens to you.

565
00:34:39,224 --> 00:34:41,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it cannot be taken away from you.

566
00:34:42,185 --> 00:34:46,485
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, pleasant feeling has arisen, you're subjected to it, and then it disappears.

567
00:34:46,605 --> 00:34:48,146
[SPEAKER_01]: It's taken away from you.

568
00:34:49,286 --> 00:34:59,188
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you developed your mind in regard to manifestation, in regard to arising of anything, feeling, perception, intention, sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, thought, whatever,

569
00:35:00,522 --> 00:35:04,145
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dependent on the content of it, but it's dependent on your attitude towards it.

570
00:35:04,746 --> 00:35:08,349
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize you will be immovable in regard to anything and everything.

571
00:35:09,329 --> 00:35:15,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the most extreme examples that are listed in the suttas, will be killed and butchered, and your mind, composure, will not change.

572
00:35:19,763 --> 00:35:31,008
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's possible, because your practice has ceased to pertain to the circumstances, to the senses, but it started looking at the nature of it, in regard to which you train your immovability, imperturbability.

573
00:35:32,289 --> 00:35:35,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And that training starts when you're completely unenlightened, patujana.

574
00:35:37,391 --> 00:35:41,313
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't, you don't magically slot into it, when you develop the right view.

575
00:35:41,613 --> 00:35:43,914
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop the right view, because you've been training rightly.

576
00:35:45,835 --> 00:35:46,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you say,

577
00:35:49,676 --> 00:36:01,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's quite common to, I think, for people to think that practicing the dhamma involves... not thinking, involves kind of... thinking... Just do it.

578
00:36:01,582 --> 00:36:02,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking is the problem.

579
00:36:02,623 --> 00:36:03,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Just do it.

580
00:36:03,143 --> 00:36:05,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're overthinking these things, just... Yeah.

581
00:36:05,525 --> 00:36:08,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the question you should immediately ask yourself there is...

582
00:36:09,507 --> 00:36:13,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's just about doing it, where is the motivation for doing rooted?

583
00:36:13,950 --> 00:36:17,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it rooted in doing, or is it rooted in thought behind your doing?

584
00:36:17,072 --> 00:36:17,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so...

585
00:36:17,833 --> 00:36:26,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are ignoring thought on account of your doing, means you ignore the reason for motivation, which means you ignore what action is, and somehow you're hoping to free yourself from action.

586
00:36:26,499 --> 00:36:38,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, if someone wants to really start practicing in line with the dhamma, if they're not already, which you probably are if you're listening to these videos, but...

587
00:36:39,820 --> 00:36:44,806
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about, kind of, you know, I do need to start thinking about my motivation.

588
00:36:45,626 --> 00:36:46,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where dhamma is?

589
00:36:47,128 --> 00:36:47,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

590
00:36:49,410 --> 00:36:50,772
[SPEAKER_01]: My motivation for it...

591
00:36:50,892 --> 00:36:53,415
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not justified in doing anything or not doing anything.

592
00:36:53,875 --> 00:36:55,357
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no justification for anything.

593
00:36:56,771 --> 00:37:00,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever I do or whatever I choose not to do, it's because I made a choice for which I'm responsible.

594
00:37:01,173 --> 00:37:04,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I'm not justified or absolved from that responsibility.

595
00:37:05,295 --> 00:37:06,576
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to practice dhamma.

596
00:37:06,656 --> 00:37:07,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

597
00:37:07,056 --> 00:37:07,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

598
00:37:07,516 --> 00:37:08,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I want to be free from suffering.

599
00:37:08,977 --> 00:37:09,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

600
00:37:09,557 --> 00:37:09,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

601
00:37:09,817 --> 00:37:12,279
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's important is my responsibility.

602
00:37:12,539 --> 00:37:13,819
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm responsible for what's important.

603
00:37:13,839 --> 00:37:20,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, recognizing that responsibility, not running away from it, not covering it up because it's unpleasant, accepting that uneasiness.

604
00:37:20,783 --> 00:37:22,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're responsible, yet you're not in control.

605
00:37:23,024 --> 00:37:23,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

606
00:37:23,284 --> 00:37:24,605
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very uneasy situation.

607
00:37:24,645 --> 00:37:24,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

608
00:37:25,525 --> 00:37:30,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, if I tell you, oh, if something happens to such and such person, if they do something stupid, you pay the price.

609
00:37:31,069 --> 00:37:31,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

610
00:37:31,770 --> 00:37:31,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

611
00:37:32,971 --> 00:37:34,191
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically what saṃsāra is.

612
00:37:34,211 --> 00:37:35,132
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not at fault.

613
00:37:35,152 --> 00:37:35,893
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible.

614
00:37:35,933 --> 00:37:36,793
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what saṃsāra is.

615
00:37:36,813 --> 00:37:37,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what existence is.

616
00:37:37,754 --> 00:37:37,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

617
00:37:37,934 --> 00:37:38,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

618
00:37:38,174 --> 00:37:38,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

619
00:37:38,855 --> 00:37:40,076
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good way of putting it.

620
00:37:40,996 --> 00:37:46,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize... recognizing that existential responsibility is the gateway for the direction of the dhamma.

621
00:37:46,761 --> 00:37:46,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

622
00:37:47,021 --> 00:37:49,323
[SPEAKER_01]: You can spend 50 years practicing the dhamma.

623
00:37:49,363 --> 00:37:54,787
[SPEAKER_01]: If you haven't started that practice through that gateway, there is no dhamma in your practice.

624
00:37:59,026 --> 00:38:10,046
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that upsets you, hearing that, well, it just confirms that you haven't developed freedom from suffering, you haven't developed imperturbability in regard to circumstances, which is words of others.

625
00:38:13,347 --> 00:38:15,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is my practice freeing me from suffering?

626
00:38:16,248 --> 00:38:28,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by controlling the circumstances, preventing the exposure to things they might have said to me, but through me being unable to move, unable to suffer, unable to despair, on account of things I cannot escape.

627
00:38:29,493 --> 00:38:31,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you will not need an escape anymore.

628
00:38:31,994 --> 00:38:36,996
[SPEAKER_01]: As in trying to outrun saṃsāṃs, mādhya-sthācchas, deny them through refusing to think about them, and so on.

629
00:38:37,376 --> 00:38:38,797
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot suffer either way.

630
00:38:39,587 --> 00:38:41,288
[SPEAKER_01]: That's truly the burdenless.

631
00:38:41,869 --> 00:38:42,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I don't need to do anything.

632
00:38:43,090 --> 00:38:44,051
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no burden for me.

633
00:38:44,871 --> 00:38:46,233
[SPEAKER_01]: When I see this, I must do this.

634
00:38:46,593 --> 00:38:48,855
[SPEAKER_01]: When I think that, I must run this way.

635
00:38:49,055 --> 00:38:54,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you say that's the kind of... that sense of self is almost... that's what it is.

636
00:38:54,239 --> 00:38:54,980
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that escape.

637
00:38:55,340 --> 00:38:56,061
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that kind of...

638
00:38:59,198 --> 00:39:00,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Safety...

639
00:39:00,339 --> 00:39:00,959
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a product.

640
00:39:01,239 --> 00:39:01,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

641
00:39:01,659 --> 00:39:03,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Of your dependence on looking for that.

642
00:39:03,340 --> 00:39:05,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the assumption that there is some... Yeah.

643
00:39:05,921 --> 00:39:06,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Some... Yeah.

644
00:39:06,421 --> 00:39:07,201
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the direct product.

645
00:39:07,221 --> 00:39:08,242
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what means sense of self.

646
00:39:08,282 --> 00:39:09,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Self is not a problem.

647
00:39:09,202 --> 00:39:09,742
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom.

648
00:39:09,802 --> 00:39:09,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

649
00:39:10,843 --> 00:39:12,103
[SPEAKER_01]: And you want to approve that symptom.

650
00:39:12,143 --> 00:39:13,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you stop maintaining it.

651
00:39:13,744 --> 00:39:15,845
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop ignoring saṅkhara.

652
00:39:16,105 --> 00:39:16,285
Yeah.

653
00:39:17,390 --> 00:39:23,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Being the dukkha and being anicca and being dukkha, as in being not in my control and being uneasy.

654
00:39:23,974 --> 00:39:29,496
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop ignoring non-control and uneasiness, you will stop maintaining, this is mine, this is myself.

655
00:39:31,938 --> 00:39:35,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily think about, oh, now I must practice anatta.

656
00:39:35,319 --> 00:39:40,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta will be the result of you not supporting atta, through your own actions and thinking and so on.

657
00:39:44,284 --> 00:39:45,805
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start undoing contradictions.

658
00:39:46,250 --> 00:39:46,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

659
00:39:47,111 --> 00:39:51,634
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will result in the right view, and then that will result in arhantship.

660
00:39:51,654 --> 00:39:54,056
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fundamental contradiction, sorry, is what we said.

661
00:39:54,596 --> 00:39:57,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't need to chase down all the contradictions in the world.

662
00:39:57,779 --> 00:39:58,079
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

663
00:39:58,099 --> 00:40:05,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Start with the ones that are, like, more obvious to you, based on whatever faculties you have, but fundamentally, bring them all home, so to speak.

664
00:40:05,845 --> 00:40:06,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

665
00:40:06,085 --> 00:40:09,368
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the fundamental contradiction of this body?

666
00:40:10,820 --> 00:40:13,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that I assume control over it, and yet I'm not in control of it.

667
00:40:14,282 --> 00:40:19,165
[SPEAKER_01]: That you assume... and, like, the life that depends upon the operating body?

668
00:40:19,205 --> 00:40:19,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

669
00:40:20,286 --> 00:40:21,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Can that be in your control?

670
00:40:21,307 --> 00:40:21,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I would say.

671
00:40:22,968 --> 00:40:27,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, is that... is that the context that you have at the back of your mind throughout your day?

672
00:40:27,590 --> 00:40:30,732
[SPEAKER_01]: That you're not in control on your own body, let alone of your own life?

673
00:40:31,273 --> 00:40:34,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is that a context that you kind of drift away from and forget about and ignore?

674
00:40:36,092 --> 00:40:36,973
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes.

675
00:40:37,013 --> 00:40:40,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to make extra effort towards ignorance.

676
00:40:40,237 --> 00:40:42,059
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is ignore the right thing.

677
00:40:42,599 --> 00:40:44,782
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you're investing in ignorance already.

678
00:40:45,462 --> 00:40:49,146
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is lose the sight of the context that you're not in control of this body.

679
00:40:50,608 --> 00:40:53,071
[SPEAKER_01]: If you lose that sight, automatically, you're assuming control.

680
00:40:53,571 --> 00:40:55,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, you're acting out of greed, aversion, delusion.

681
00:40:57,576 --> 00:40:59,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just...

682
00:40:59,938 --> 00:41:14,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's learning not to tolerate contradictions, but also learning to recognize them, because you've, for so long, you've allowed them to be there without actually... And you will recognize them when you learn how to endure them, and not give in to them.

683
00:41:15,590 --> 00:41:15,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

684
00:41:16,951 --> 00:41:19,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you learn to endure the uneasiness,

685
00:41:21,779 --> 00:41:25,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, the first uneasiness you're going to get is the uneasiness of starting to keep the precepts.

686
00:41:26,342 --> 00:41:34,929
[SPEAKER_00]: And you might... you might, first of all, that those contradictions might be gross, might be quite obvious, but maybe that's why you need to stop.

687
00:41:34,949 --> 00:41:44,236
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you're saying is like, well, there will be more deep-rooted... And if you wonder where the contradictions are, well, just ask yourself, have you been acting out of desire recently?

688
00:41:44,556 --> 00:41:46,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you committed any act by body, speech or mind out of desire?

689
00:41:47,098 --> 00:41:48,359
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your contradiction already.

690
00:41:49,178 --> 00:41:51,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire implies ownership, implies that result.

691
00:41:51,864 --> 00:41:52,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you feel the shame?

692
00:41:53,106 --> 00:41:53,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

693
00:41:54,375 --> 00:41:55,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see the danger of that design?

694
00:41:55,616 --> 00:41:55,756
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

695
00:41:55,836 --> 00:41:57,918
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another contradiction, because it's dangerous.

696
00:41:58,418 --> 00:42:05,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Here you are, riding on this body, as if you created it, you're in control, yet you have no idea even how components operate.

697
00:42:05,704 --> 00:42:07,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And if something interferes with it, that's it.

698
00:42:08,046 --> 00:42:09,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Your ride is gone.

699
00:42:09,627 --> 00:42:10,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It's finished.

700
00:42:10,628 --> 00:42:11,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that.

701
00:42:11,289 --> 00:42:13,170
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why death is so terrifying for people.

702
00:42:13,210 --> 00:42:18,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because death is terrifying itself, but because you literally, your entire existence, ignores it.

703
00:42:18,835 --> 00:42:20,616
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you ignore it, the more power it has.

704
00:42:27,670 --> 00:42:32,411
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that transparency is developed sufficiently enough, then, literally, it becomes obvious.

705
00:42:33,371 --> 00:42:34,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpatti is obvious.

706
00:42:35,311 --> 00:42:36,952
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the declaration of satipaṭṭhāna.

707
00:42:37,172 --> 00:42:37,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

708
00:42:38,172 --> 00:42:42,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature to manifest, for that very reason, it's not in my control.

709
00:42:42,933 --> 00:42:43,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's manifested.

710
00:42:44,073 --> 00:42:44,913
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it will cease.

711
00:42:45,993 --> 00:42:48,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

712
00:42:48,474 --> 00:42:53,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you just cultivate that same thing and any traces of any desire, any little contradictions or discrepancies, gone.

